1. Nf3 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 b6 4. Bg2 Bb7 5. O-O Be7 6. Nc3 O-O 7. Re1 {[This variation has become very popular recently with lot of games, played with it in the tournaments of al levels. Black in the position has 3 main answers at his disposal: 7...c5, 7...d5 and 7...e4. White has a small but long-lasting pluses in the variations 7...d5 8.exd5 xd5 9.d4 and 8...exd5 9.d4, while Black position remains very passive after 7...c5 8.e4, followed by d2-d4 ("hedgehog" structure). The author of this lines has tried both of the lines with 7...c5 and 7...d5 before switching to the variation with 7...e4.]} Ne4 $1 {[The strongest move in the position according to the author of this lines. The idea of this move lies in the desire to exchange the knights, freeing the space in the position, thus making the defence much more easier. In total I have played 6 games with the variation, having won 2 with 4 draws.]} 8. Nxe4 {[Other moves don't promise White any advantage.]} (8. d4 Nxc3 9. bxc3 Nc6 10. e4 Na5 11. Nd2 d6 $11 12. Bb2 e5 13. Qe2 Ba6 14. f4 Qd7 15. a4 Bf6 16. Bf1 Rfe8 17. Qf2 {½-½ Popov,V-Biriukov,O/St Petersburg 1998/CBM 65 (17)}) (8. Qc2 Nxc3 (8... d5 $5 {Schandorff,L-Ochsner,T/Copenhagen 1997/EXT 99}) 9. bxc3 (9. Ng5 {(Boxall-Illingworth,L/England 1951)} Bxg5 10. Bxb7 $6 Nxe2+ 11. Rxe2 Nc6 12. Qe4 f5 $17) (9. Qxc3 c5 $11) 9... f5 (9... Nc6 $5 {a5, ×c4}) 10. d3 Bf6 11. a4 Nc6 $13 {Tratar Marko (SLO)-Drei Andrea (ITA)/Opatija (Croatia) 1999.}) 8... Bxe4 9. d3 Bb7 10. e4 ({After the only alternative to the text} 10. d4 {White threatens to fully occupy the centre after e2-e4, then he would have a large advantage. Thus the following Black move is not so difficult to predict.} {[#]} Be4 $1 {The position now is equal. Please, note, that in comparison with the variation 1.d4 f6 2.c4 e6 3.f3 b6 4.g3 b7 5.g2 e7 6.0-0 0-0 7.c3 e4 8.xe4 xe4 White has won a tempo (he has played an extra move f1-e1), but it doesn't change the assessment. White is not able to drive the black bishop from the square e4 without bringing lot of harm to his position. Here follows the selection of the games.} 11. d5 (11. Nh4 Bxg2 12. Nxg2 d5 (12... d6 13. e4 $14 {Vaganian,R-Dokhoian,Y/Tilburg 1994/CBM 44}) 13. Qa4 dxc4 14. Qxc4 c5 15. Be3 Nd7 16. dxc5 Bxc5 {½-½ Savchenko,Stanislav-Tiviakov,Sergei/Jakarta (Indonesia) (6) 1997}) (11. Bf1 $6 c5 12. Nd2 Bc6 13. dxc5 bxc5 14. b3 a5 15. Bb2 a4 16. e4 Na6 $15 {Sokolin,Leonid M-Tiviakov,Sergei/New York (USA) (8) 1998}) (11. Bf4 Bf6 12. Qd2 d6 13. Rad1 Nd7 14. Qe3 d5 $11 15. b4 c6 16. Rc1 b5 17. cxb5 cxb5 18. Bc7 Qe8 19. Ne5 Bxg2 20. Kxg2 Nb6 21. Bxb6 axb6 22. Nc6 Rc8 23. Ne5 Qe7 24. Rxc8 Rxc8 25. Rc1 Rxc1 26. Qxc1 Qxb4 27. Qc8+ {½-½ Hjartarson Johann (ISL)-Stefansson Hannes (ISL)/Skakping Islands (Iceland) 1995 (27)}) (11. Bh3 c5 12. d5 exd5 13. Nd2 Bf6 14. Nxe4 dxe4 15. Bf5 Re8 16. Rb1 Qe7 17. Bf4 Nc6 18. Qxd7 Nd4 19. Qxe7 Rxe7 20. Bg4 g6 21. Bd6 {½-½ Kogan Artur (ISR)-Ikonnikov Vyacheslav (RUS)/Paris (France) 1998 (21)}) 11... exd5 12. cxd5 Bf6 $1 {(Black must keep the eye on the pawn b2, preventing the development of the bishop c1.)} ({after} 12... c5 13. Bf4 d6 14. Rc1 Bf6 $14 {White is slightly better} (14... Nd7 $6 15. Nd4 $1 Bxg2 16. Nc6 Qe8 17. Kxg2 Nb8 18. Nxe7+ Qxe7 19. Rc3 $16 {Csom Istvan (HUN)-Spassov Liuben (BUL)/Hamburg (Germany) 1977})) 13. Nd2 Bxg2 14. Kxg2 Re8 15. Rb1 (15. e4 {17/37}) (15. Qc2 {17/37}) 15... Na6 16. f3 c6 $1 17. dxc6 (17. Ne4 $6 Rxe4 $1 18. fxe4 Qe7 19. Qd3 Nc5 20. Qf3 Nxe4 $15 {17/37}) 17... dxc6 18. Ne4 Be7 19. Qa4 Nb4 20. a3 b5 21. Qb3 Qd5 {½-½ Petrosian,T-Portisch,L/Palma de Mallorca (m/11) 17/37 1974}) 10... c5 11. d4 {[#]} d5 $2 {[A serious mistake: according to the principles of the chess strategy, the side falling behind in the development should refrain from the complications! It is clear to see that it is White whose pieces are better developed, moreover he has a control over the centre. White bishop on g2 has potential chances to become bad. Black in his further play must try to exploit that. In the text game after 12.exd5 exd5 13.e5 White bishop g2 becomes very strong. I cannot understand why Jan Timman, the player of a great strength has made that move. Probably he was going to play 11...d6, a worthy alternative to 11...cxd4, although leading to the position where White has a space advantage, but suddenly had chosen 11...d5, or his decision was based on miscalculation. 'N?' Gelfand,B;Huzman,A.} (11... d6 12. d5 (12. b3 cxd4 (12... Bf6 13. Bb2 Nc6 (13... Qc7 14. Qe2 e5 15. d5 Nd7 16. h4 $14 {Gyorkos,L-Jakab,A/HUN 1999/EXT 2000}) 14. e5 dxe5 15. dxe5 Be7 16. Nd2 $14 {Banas,J-Rakay,K/Rimavska Sobota 1975/EXT 99}) (12... Nd7 13. Bb2 a6 (13... cxd4 14. Nxd4 a6 $14) 14. Qd2 Qc7 15. Rad1 Rad8 16. h4 $14 {Tregubov Pavel V (RUS)-Stefansson Hannes (ISL)/Linares (Spain) 1996}) 13. Nxd4 a6 {leads the game to the position studied after 11...cxd4 12.xd4 a6 13.b3 d6}) (12. Bd2 Nd7 (12... cxd4 13. Nxd4 Nc6 $14) 13. Bc3 Bf6 14. Rc1 Qc7 15. Qd2 Rfd8 16. d5 $14 {Zuchovitzky-Popov/corr 1975}) 12... exd5 (12... e5 13. Be3 $14 {Hutters Tomas (DEN)-Pilgaard Kim (DEN)/Copenhagen (Denmark) 1994} (13. Bd2 Bc8 14. Bc3 Bg4 15. h3 Bh5 16. Qd3 Nd7 17. Nd2 Bg5 18. Rf1 Bxd2 19. Qxd2 $14 {Piven,I-Ikonnikov,V/Werfen 1996/EXT 2000})) 13. cxd5 (13. exd5 Bf6 14. Qb3 Nd7 15. Bf4 Qc7 16. Re2 Rfe8 17. Rae1 Rxe2 18. Rxe2 Nf8 19. g4 Ng6 20. Bg3 h6 21. a4 Rd8 22. h4 Bc8 23. g5 hxg5 24. hxg5 Be7 25. Qe3 Bf8 26. Nh4 Nxh4 27. Bxh4 g6 28. Bh3 Bg7 29. b3 {½-½ Agdestein,S-Stefansson,H/Reykjavik 66/(22) 1996}) 13... Nd7 14. Bf4 b5 (14... Qc7 15. Rc1 Ne5 16. Nd4 $14 {Sale,S-Ascic,P/Pula 1999/EXT 2000}) 15. Rc1 $14 {Krivoshey,S-Oral,T/Prievidza 1997/EXT 99}) ({After} 11... cxd4 {(the strongest move in the position)} 12. Nxd4 {Black has 2 main answers in the position: 12...c6 and 12...a6. 12...d6, followed by a7-a6 later mostly transposes into the line with 12...a6. There is nothing wrong with 12...a6, but 13.d4-b5 is not a threat. That's why Black must refrain from playing it for as long as possible, first developing his knight b8.} Nc6 {Now White in the position has 5 different moves at his disposal.} (12... a6 13. Qg4 $5 (13. b3 d6 14. Ba3 (14. Bb2 Nd7 (14... Re8 15. Rc1 Nd7 16. Qe2 Rc8 17. Rc2 Rc7 18. Rd1 Qa8 19. Rcd2 Bf8 20. f4 Nf6 $11 {Spraggett Kevin (CAN)-Arencibia Walter (CUB)/Habana (Cuba) 1997 (75).}) 15. Qg4 Bf6 16. Rad1 Qc7 17. f4 Rfe8 18. Qe2 Rad8 19. Ba3 Nc5 $15 {e5, e, ×e4, Kindl,P-Fritsch,R/Wuerttemberg 1979/EXT 99}) 14... Qc7 15. Qe2 Nd7 16. Rad1 Rad8 $11 {Jean Olivier (FRA)-Atlas Valeri (LIE)/France 1999}) (13. Bf4 d6 14. Nf3 Qc7 15. Rc1 Rd8 16. b4 Nd7 17. Qb3 e5 18. Bg5 Bxg5 19. Nxg5 h6 20. Nf3 a5 {½-½ Kushch Nikolai (UKR)-Karpman Vadim (UKR)/Smolensk (Russia) 1991 (20)}) 13... Re8 $1 14. b3 Bf8 15. Bb2 d6 16. e5 $5 (16. Rad1 {32/19}) 16... Bxg2 17. Kxg2 dxe5 18. Rxe5 Nd7 19. Re2 Qc7 20. Nf3 Qb7 21. Qe4 Qxe4 22. Rxe4 b5 $11 {Sunye-Neto,J-Andersson,U/Brasilia 32/19 1981}) {[#]} 13. Be3 ({after} 13. Bf4 $146 {Black has 3 different ways to equalise.} Bc5 (13... Rc8 $5 14. Re2 Nxd4 15. Qxd4 Bc5 16. Qd3 f6 17. Rd1 Rf7 18. b3 e5 19. Be3 Qe7 20. Bxc5 Qxc5 $11 {Zhu Chen (CHN)-Tiviakov Sergei (RUS)/Beijing (China) 1998}) (13... Nxd4 $5 14. Qxd4 Bc5 {} 15. Qd3 Qe7 16. e5 Bxg2 17. Kxg2 f6 18. Rad1 fxe5 19. Rxe5 d6 20. Re2 Qb7+ 21. Qe4 Qxe4+ 22. Rxe4 Rf6 23. Be3 Bxe3 24. Rxe3 Rd8 25. Red3 e5 26. Rd5 Re6 $11 {Gurevich,M-Roeder,M/ BL2-W 1996}) 14. Nxc6 Bxc6 15. Bd6 Bxd6 16. Qxd6 Qb8 17. Rad1 Qxd6 18. Rxd6 Rfd8 19. Red1 Kf8 20. e5 Bxg2 21. Kxg2 Ke7 $11 {Ibragimov,Ildar-Tiviakov,Sergei/Elista (Russia) (1) 1997, 69/(32)}) (13. b3 {can be strongly met by} Nxd4 14. Qxd4 e5 $11 {The position is equal. The weakness of the pawn d7 is compensated by the white bad bishop g2.} ({or} 14... Bc5 15. Qc3 (15. Qd2 Qe7 16. a3 a5 17. Bb2 Bc6 18. Qd3 f6 19. Re2 Rab8 20. a4 Rbd8 21. Rd1 Qf7 {½-½ Davies,N-Schandorff,L/DEN-chB 1988 (33)}) 15... f5 $1 {} (15... Qe7 16. a3 $1 (16. Bb2 f5 17. exf5 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 Bb4 19. Qe5 Bxe1 20. Rxe1 Rae8 21. fxe6 dxe6 $13 {Vaganian Rafael A (ARM)-Vitolinsh Alvis (LAT)/Yerevan (Armenia) 1980}) 16... a5 17. Bb2 f6 18. Re2 Bc6 19. a4 Qf7 20. Ba3 Qh5 21. Rd2 Rf7 22. Bxc5 Qxc5 23. Rad1 Raf8 24. Qd4 Qxd4 25. Rxd4 Rb8 26. Rd6 Rb7 27. f4 $14 {Vaganian,R-Yermolinsky,A/Tilburg 1993 (54)}) 16. exf5 Bxg2 17. Kxg2 Rxf5 18. f4 Qf6 19. Bd2 d5 20. Rad1 dxc4 21. Qxc4 Rd5 22. Bc3 Qf7 $11 {Schlosser,P-Yermolinsky,A/Koop Tjuchem op 1996}) (14... Qc7 15. Bb2 Bf6 16. e5 Be7 $11 {is equal according to Stohl.}) 15. Qd2 a5 16. Rd1 Bc6 17. a3 Bc5 18. Bb2 Qe7 19. Bc3 Bxa3 20. Bxe5 Qxe5 21. Rxa3 Rfe8 22. Ra2 h5 23. Qe3 Qc5 24. Qxc5 bxc5 25. Rda1 Bxe4 26. Rxa5 Bxg2 27. Kxg2 Rxa5 28. Rxa5 d6 {½-½ Tregubov,Pavel V-Tiviakov,Sergei/Krasnodar (Russia) (5) 1997}) (13. Nb5 d6 14. Bf4 (14. Be3 Qd7 15. Qe2 Rfc8 16. Rad1 Ne5 17. b3 a6 18. Nc3 b5 $13 {Akesson,R-Fedder,S/Copenhagen 1982/MCL}) 14... Ne5 $11 {is equal.}) ({after} 13. e5 a6 14. Bf4 Qc7 15. Nf3 {Black must free himself by means of} Rfd8 $5 {, followed by d7-d6} (15... b5 $6 {is not good for Black. E.g.} 16. Rc1 Rfc8 17. Ng5 Bxg5 18. Bxg5 $16 {Wirthensohn Heinz (SUI)-Zueger Beat (SUI)/Leukerbad (Switzerland) 1992}) (15... Rad8 $5 {is possible as well.})) 13... a6 (13... Rc8 14. Nb5 d6 {is very risky for Black because of} 15. e5 dxe5 16. Nxa7 Nxa7 17. Bxb7 {. Although Black managed to hold the position, White can, probably, improve his play.} Rxc4 18. Qxd8 Rxd8 19. Bxb6 Rb8 20. Ba6 Ra4 21. Bxa7 Ra8 22. Bb5 Rb4 23. a4 Rxa7 24. Rac1 g6 25. Rc2 e4 26. Kg2 Bf6 {½-½ Damljanovic Branko (YUG)-Yudasin Leonid (ISR)/Belgrade (Yugoslavia) 1998 (26)}) 14. Rc1 Rc8 {Black refrains from playing d7-d6, because it is much more easier to defend it on d7 and because the pawn on d6 limits the activity of the black dark-squared bishop. The best square for the bishop is c5. Here} 15. b3 $5 {has yet to be tried in the tournament practice. Other moves are not dangerous for Black either.} (15. h3 Qc7 16. b3 Bc5 17. Qd3 f5 $5 (17... Nxd4 18. Bxd4 Bxd4 19. Qxd4 d6 20. Rcd1 Rfd8 21. Re3 $1 Kf8 (21... Rd7 22. Qd2 Rcd8 23. Rd3 $14 {59/59}) 22. Qd2 e5 23. h4 (23. Rd3 $5 Ke7 24. Qg5+ Kf8 25. Qf5 $1 Kg8 26. f4 $14 {59/59}) 23... h6 24. Rd3 Ke7 25. g4 b5 26. cxb5 axb5 27. g5 Qc2 $1 28. Rxd6 (28. Qe3 Rc3 $1 $11 {59/59}) 28... Qxd2 $142 $5 29. R6xd2 Rxd2 30. Rxd2 hxg5 31. hxg5 Rc1+ 32. Kh2 b4 $1 $14 {Ribli,Z-Atlas,V/Deutschland 59/59 1994}) 18. exf5 Ne5 19. Qf1 Bxg2 20. Kxg2 Qb7+ 21. f3 exf5 $13 {59/59}) (15. Qe2 Qc7 16. Red1 Nxd4 17. Bxd4 e5 18. Be3 Bc6 19. Rc3 Qb7 20. Rcd3 b5 21. c5 b4 $15 {Chuchelov,V-Tiviakov,S/Haarlem 1999})) 12. exd5 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A: ' '} exd5 13. Ne5 {[Now Black is in trouble. White is threatening to win the pawn d5.] Gelfand,B;Huzman,A: ' '} (13. dxc5 $5 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} dxc4 (13... Bxc5 14. Ng5 (14. Ne5 Nd7 15. cxd5 Nxe5 16. Rxe5 Qf6 17. Re2) 14... Qf6 (14... h6 15. Nh3) 15. Qc2 $16) 14. Qc2 (14. Qxd8 Bxd8 15. Ne5 Bxg2 16. Kxg2 bxc5 17. Nxc4 $14) 14... Nd7 (14... Nc6 15. cxb6 Qxb6 16. Qxc4 Bf6 17. Be3 $18) 15. cxb6 Nxb6 16. Bf4 $16) 13... cxd4 {[The only move.]} 14. Qxd4 Na6 {[Indirectly defending the pawn. After both 15.xd5 and 15.cxd5 captures Black plays 15...b4 with a double threat to win the pawn back or to play 16...c2.] '!?' Gelfand,B;Huzman,A.} (14... Bc5 15. Qg4 (15. Qd1) 15... Qf6 {can be met by} (15... Nc6 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 16. Nxc6 Bxc6 17. cxd5 $18) 16. Nd3 ({or} 16. Bf4) (16. Bf4)) (14... Bf6 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 15. cxd5 Re8 (15... Nc6 16. Nxc6 Bxd4 17. Nxd8 Rfxd8 18. Rd1 Bf6 19. a4 $16) 16. Bf4 Nd7 (16... Nc6 17. dxc6 Qxd4 18. cxb7 $18) 17. g4 $16 (17. h4 h6 18. h5 Bxe5 19. Bxe5 Qg5 20. Bf4 Qxh5 $14)) 15. Qg4 {[Without any direct threat, since 16.h6 f6 17.d7 can be met by 17...c8. But the pin along the diagonal h1-a8 is very annoying and almost paralyses Black.] '?!' Gelfand,B;Huzman,A. Gelfand,B;Huzman,A: ' '} (15. Rd1 $5 {is a worthy alternative to the text.}) (15. Bf4 $5 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} Nc7 (15... Bc5 16. Qd1 Nc7 17. Nd3 (17. Qg4 $5 $36) 17... Bd6 18. Qh5 $16) 16. Qd2 $16 (16. Rad1 Bb4)) (15. cxd5 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} Nb4 16. Rd1 (16. Qg4 Nc2 17. d6 (17. Bh6 Bf6 18. Be4 $2 Bc8 (18... Nxe1 19. Bxg7 Bxg7 20. Qf5 Nf3+ $3 21. Nxf3 Re8 $19)) 17... Nxe1 18. Bxb7 Qxd6 19. Bxa8 Qxe5 20. Bf4 Qa5 $132) 16... Nc2 17. Qg4 Nxa1 18. Nc6 Bxc6 19. dxc6 Qc8 20. Rd7 Bf6 $13) (15. Rd1 $5 {(Gelfand,B;Huzman,A)}) 15... Nb4 {[Threating to win an exchange after 16...c2.]} (15... Nc7 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 16. Bf4) 16. Rd1 Qc8 {'!' Gelfand,B;Huzman,A.} (16... Qc7 17. Bf4 Bd6 {loses after} 18. cxd5 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A: ' '} Bxe5 19. d6) 17. Bd2 {'!' Gelfand,B;Huzman,A. Gelfand,B;Huzman,A: ' '} (17. cxd5 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} Rd8 18. Qf3 f6 (18... Bf6 19. Ng4) 19. Qb3 Qc2) (17. a3 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} Nc2 18. Rb1 Bd6 $1 19. Nd7 f5 $1 (19... Rd8 20. Bh3) 20. Qe2 Qxd7 21. Qxc2 f4 22. Qd3 $140 d4 $132) 17... Bd6 {[Black is still unable to free himself from the pin.] '!' Gelfand,B;Huzman,A.} (17... dxc4 18. Qxc8 $18 {loses a piece or a rook.}) (17... Qxg4 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 18. Nxg4) (17... f6 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 18. Bxb4 Bxb4 19. cxd5 Qxg4 20. Nxg4 $16) 18. Bxb4 Bxe5 19. Qxc8 (19. Qh5 $6 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} Re8 20. cxd5 Bxb2 21. Rab1 Qc2 $1 (21... Bf6) 22. Bf1 Bd4 $1) 19... Rfxc8 20. cxd5 Bxb2 {[Black managed to avoid any material loss but with a great cost. White has a passed pawn d5, very strong and dangerous, within a couple of moves it will be on d7.]} 21. Rab1 Bf6 (21... Rc2 $5 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 22. Be4 (22. Be7 Bf6 23. d6 Bxg2 24. Kxg2 Re2 25. Bxf6 gxf6 26. Rbc1 Rd8 27. a3 Kf8 28. Rc7 Re6 29. d7 a5 $14) (22. d6 Bxg2 23. d7 Bf6 24. Kxg2 Rd8 25. Re1 (25. Rd3 h6 26. Re1 Rc7 27. Re8+ Kh7 28. Rxd8 Bxd8) 25... h6 26. Re8+ (26. Rbd1 Rc7 27. Re8+ Kh7 28. Rxd8 Bxd8) 26... Kh7 27. Rxd8 Bxd8) 22... Rc4 23. Bxh7+ Kxh7 24. Rxb2 Rd8 25. d6 $14) (21... Bc3 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 22. Bh3 Rc4 23. Be7) 22. Bh3 Rc4 $2 ({I would have played} 22... Rc2 {and if} 23. d6 {, then} (23. a4) 23... Rxa2 24. d7 {hoping later to give up the bishop for the passed pawn with some drawing chances. But 23.a3 or 23.a4 are, probably, stronger than 23.d6. Gelfand,B;Huzman,A: ' '} (24. d7 {(Gelfand,B;Huzman,A)} Rd8 25. Bd6 $16)) 23. d6 $18 Bf3 {'?' Gelfand,B;Huzman,A.} ({after} 23... a5 24. Be1 {the pawn b6 is a target.}) ({but} 23... Rd8 $5 {deserved serious attention, planning to meet} 24. d7 {by} Bc6 {with some resistance.}) (23... Be4 $1 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 24. Bf1 (24. Rdc1 Bxb1 25. Rxc4 Bxa2 26. Rc7 a5 27. d7 Rd8 28. Bd6 Be6) 24... Rcc8 25. Rbc1 (25. Bd3 Bf3 26. Re1 Rd8) 25... Rxc1 26. Rxc1 Bf5 27. Ba6 $1 Bh3 28. Rd1 $14) 24. Rdc1 Rxc1+ (24... Bd5 $5 {(Gelfand,B;Huzman,A)}) 25. Rxc1 Rd8 26. d7 {[Now the game is over. Black can do nothing against the pawn d7.]} h5 (26... Bd5 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 27. Rc8 Be6 (27... Bxa2 28. Bd6 Be6 29. Bxe6 fxe6 30. Bc7 $18) 28. Bxe6 fxe6 29. Bc3 Be7 30. Rc7 $16) 27. Rc7 {'+-' Gelfand,B;Huzman,A.} a6 {'?' Gelfand,B;Huzman,A.} (27... a5 {Gelfand,B;Huzman,A} 28. Bd6 $1 (28. Bd2 Kf8 (28... Bg4 29. Bxg4 hxg4 30. Bf4 g5 31. Be3 Kf8 32. Bxb6 Ke7 33. Rc6) 29. Be3 Bg4 30. Bxg4 hxg4 31. Bxb6 Ke7 32. Bxa5 Rxd7 $14) 28... Bg4 29. Bxg4 hxg4 30. f3 $18) 28. Bc3 Kf8 29. Rc8 Bg5 30. Bd4 Ke7 31. Rxd8 1-0

