1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. a3 Na5 (9... Be6 {There is a point to this move, since in the símilar line 9.h3 Be6 White´s best is 10.d4 xb3 11.axb3. Now taking back with the a- is no longer possible.} 10. d4 (10. Bxe6 fxe6 11. d4 Nh5 (11... Qc8 12. Nbd2 Nd7 13. a4 Nd8 $13 {½-½ Kharlov,A-Nenashev,A/Moscow 1992/CBM 33 (42)}) 12. d5 Na5 13. b4 Nc4 14. dxe6 Qe8 $36 {0-1 Azaric,S-Ziatdinov,R/Belgrade 1989/EXT 97 (40)}) 10... Bxb3 (10... Bg4 11. Be3 exd4 12. cxd4 d5 13. e5 Ne4 14. Nbd2 Qd7 15. Qb1 $14 {0-1 Suetin,A-Smagin,S/Moscow 1982/MCD (77)}) 11. Qxb3 Qb8 (11... d5 12. dxe5 Nxe4 13. Nbd2 Nc5 14. Qd1 d4 15. cxd4 Nxd4 16. b4 Nd3 17. Re4 Ne6 18. Nf1 Nxe5 {0-1 Koehn,P-Smejkal,J/Germany 1999/GER-chT (18)}) (11... Nd7 12. Nbd2 Bf6 13. d5 Na5 14. Qc2 Nb6 15. Nf1 c5 16. dxc6 Qc7 17. Be3 Nac4 18. Bxb6 Nxb6 19. Ne3 Qxc6 20. Rad1 $14 {½-½ Suetin,A-Stein,L/Kiev 1964/URS-ch (47)}) 12. Nbd2 Qb6 13. d5 Na5 14. Qd1 (14. Qa2 c6 (14... c5) 15. b4 Nb7 16. Nf1 cxd5 17. exd5 Rac8 $13 {1-0 Muhutdinov,M-Shchekachev,A/Moscow 1992/EXT 97 (32)}) 14... c5 15. b4 Nb7 16. Rb1 Rfc8 17. Nf1 a5 $132 {0-1 Suetin,A-Keres,P/Tallinn 1965/URS-ch (40)}) 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 exd4 $146 {[%mdl 8] Taking with the e- is a true novelty here. The point is that Black can follow it up with g4.} (11... Qc7 12. Nbd2 (12. b4 cxb4 13. cxb4 Nc4 $13 {1-0 Suetin,A-Lengyel,L/Sarajevo 1965/MCD (67)}) 12... cxd4 {The standard way to exchange in the Ruy Lopez.} 13. cxd4 Bg4 (13... Bd7) 14. h3 Bxf3 15. Nxf3 Rac8 16. Re2 $14 {1-0 Suetin,A-Oldach,E/Bad Wildbad 1997/EXT 99 (32)}) (11... Nc6 12. Qd3 Bg4 13. Nbd2 cxd4 14. cxd4 exd4 15. Nxd4 Nxd4 16. Qxd4 Rc8 $11 {½-½ Minic,D-Milic,B/Beograd 1966/EXT 99 (20)}) 12. cxd4 Bg4 13. Nbd2 (13. d5 Nc4 14. Nbd2 Nxd2 15. Bxd2 Nd7 $13 {f6}) 13... cxd4 14. h3 Bh5 15. g4 d3 $1 (15... Nxg4 16. hxg4 Bxg4 17. Nb3 $14) (15... Bg6 16. Nxd4 d5 17. e5 Bxc2 18. Qxc2 Nd7 19. e6 $36) 16. Bxd3 Bg6 17. Nf1 (17. Nd4 d5 $11) 17... d5 18. e5 (18. exd5 Bxd3 19. Qxd3 Qxd5 20. Qxd5 Nxd5 $11) 18... Ne4 19. Ne3 Nc6 {Already the position isn´t all that comfortable for White. Short decides to break out with an imaginative manouvre.} (19... Nc4 $11) 20. Nf5 $5 (20. Qc2 Rc8 21. Nxd5 Nxe5 22. Nxe7+ Qxe7 23. Qd1 Nxd3 24. Qxd3 Rfd8 $17) (20. Bc2 Bc5 21. Bb3 Ne7 22. Nh4 $13) 20... Bxf5 21. gxf5 Qc8 22. Rxe4 $5 {[%mdl 768]} (22. Qc2 Qxf5 $1 23. Rxe4 dxe4 24. Bxe4 Qxh3 25. Qxc6 Rac8 26. Qb7 Bc5 $40) 22... dxe4 23. Bxe4 Rd8 24. Qf1 {White has a  and some -side pressure for the exchange.} Na5 25. Bf4 (25. Bxa8 Qxf5 $1 $17) 25... Qc4 {Black goes for a draw.} (25... Nb3 26. Re1 Ra7 {Preserves the material advantage, but White can shift his attention to the -side with moves like g2, h1 and g1, so Almasi´s decision is well founded.}) 26. Qxc4 Nxc4 27. Bxa8 Rxa8 28. b3 Nxa3 $11 29. Rc1 b4 30. Nd4 Bd8 31. Nc6 a5 32. Be3 (32. e6 $142) 32... h6 33. Bd4 Bg5 34. Rc5 a4 35. bxa4 Rxa4 36. e6 fxe6 37. fxe6 b3 38. e7 Bxe7 39. Nxe7+ Kf7 40. Nc6 Rxd4 41. Nxd4 b2 42. Rc7+ 1/2-1/2

