1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 {[First of all I would like to point the attention of the readers of these lines to the fact that only for the 3rd time in his history Boris Gelfand goes for Sveshnikov Variation of the Sicilian Defence. His main weapon is Najdorf with more than 110 games played. Personally I think the choice of the Sveshnikov was a mistake, which contributed much to the Boris's loss of the present game. Without much experience in the variation you should not try something new against a very strong player such as Nigel Short.]} 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e5 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Bg5 a6 8. Na3 b5 9. Nd5 Be7 10. Bxf6 Bxf6 11. c3 O-O 12. Nc2 Bg5 (12... Rb8 {is an alternative to the text, preventing White from playing 13.a4. But it has other drawbacks, which will not be discussed here.}) 13. a4 bxa4 14. Rxa4 a5 {[#]} 15. Bb5 $1 ({Until recently} 15. Bc4 {has been the main answer in the position with hundreds of games played with it. But after experiencing serious problems after 15.c4, another move - 15.b5 became very popular (even Garry Kasparov has tried it in his game against Loek van Wely in Wijk aan Zee 1999). Those curious readers wishing to learn more about the variation with 15.b5 can be redirected to the large theoretical article by Sergey Tiviakov, published in the New In Chess Yearbook 51. Although 6 months passed since the publication, the number of the games played with 15.b5 had doubled, the article still remains very up-to-date with almost all evaluations and assessments remaining unchanged.}) 15... Bb7 ({both} 15... Bd7) ({and} 15... Na7 {can not be recommended, since White has clear advantage in both cases.}) ({But} 15... Ne7 {, probably, remains the strongest move in the position with the following main line:} 16. Ncb4 ({the position after} 16. O-O Nxd5 17. Qxd5 Be6 {is equal.}) ({but} 16. Bc4 {deserves serious attention.}) 16... Be6 17. Nxe7+ Qxe7 18. Bc6 Ra7 ({both} 18... Rac8) ({and} 18... Rab8 {are not satisfactory for Black.}) 19. Nd5 Bxd5 20. Bxd5 {. White is slightly better here, but with the correct play Black is able to neutralise White plus.}) 16. O-O Ne7 {[Black's idea in the positions of a such kind is to try to exchange the knight on d5 and later try to free himself from the weak pawn d6, playing d6-d5. When the position becomes open, Black can exploit the strength of his bishops. That's why White aim here becomes obvious: he must try to keep the blockade on d5 and organise the pressure on the black pawns a5 and d6.]} {[#]} 17. Nce3 {[Not the only good move in the position.]} (17. Bc4 {deserves very serious attention. The idea is following: after the exchange of the knights, White is going to exchange the light-squared bishops, playing c4-d5. White chances here should be preferred. Here follows several games with it:} Bc6 (17... Kh8 18. Nxe7 Bxe7 19. Bd5 Qd7 20. Ne3 Bxd5 21. Nxd5 $16 {is bad for Black in Mork,K-Ross,P/Aalborg 1987/EXT 97}) 18. Ra2 Qb8 (18... Kh8 19. Nxe7 Qxe7 20. Bd5 Qb7 21. Bxc6 Qxc6 22. Qd3 $16 {with clear advantage for White in Dgebuadze Alexandre (GEO)-Nooijen Jos M M/Utrecht (Netherlands) 1999}) 19. Nce3 Nxd5 20. Nxd5 Qb7 21. b3 Kh8 22. Qd3 $14 {Brumm,C-Hoyer,T/Germany 1988/EXT 97-B}) 17... Bxe3 ({After} 17... Nxd5 18. Nxd5 Bxd5 19. Qxd5 $16 {Black doesn't have time to regroup his pieces and bring the bishop g5 for the defence of the both pawns a5 and d6.}) 18. Nxe3 Qb6 19. Qd3 f5 $6 {[I don't like this move. Black in the position has already 2 weaknesses: pawns a5 and d6. Thus, he must try to organise the freeing d6-d5. After 19...f5 Black will have the permanent headache with the pawn d6. The plan, chosen by V.Filippov in the following game, is very instructive.} (19... Rfd8 $1 20. Bc4 Bc6 21. Ra2 Qb7 22. Nd5 Bxd5 23. Bxd5 Nxd5 24. Qxd5 Qxd5 25. exd5 Kf8 $11 {the ending in the game Li Qun-Filippov,V/Shenyang 1999/EXT 2000 is equal.}) 20. exf5 $1 ({An improvement to the game Motylev,A-Tirard,H/Koszalin 1999/EXT 2000, which continued} 20. Nxf5 Nxf5 21. exf5 e4 22. Bc4+ Kh8 23. Qd4 Qxd4 24. cxd4 Rxf5 {, and White advantage is only nominal.}) 20... e4 21. Qe2 Nxf5 22. Bc4+ Kh8 23. Rd1 {[Now if we compare this position with the one before 19...f5, it is obvious to see that Black not only has not managed to improve his position, but he has even worsened it. He has a 3rd weakness in a position - the pawn e4, which limits the range of the bishop b7.]} Rae8 {[Black wants to consolidate his position, transferring the rook to e5.]} {[#]} 24. b4 $5 {[It seems to look like a strategic mistake: White exchanges the weakness in the opponent's camp. But the rules of strategy can not be applied strictly. With the help of tactics, White converts one type of the advantage into another - several moves later White wins a pawn.]} axb4 ({After} 24... Nxe3 $5 25. Qxe3 Qxe3 26. fxe3 Rc8 (26... axb4 27. Rxb4 $16 {×d6}) 27. bxa5 d5 $16 {, followed by c8:c3, Black keeps drawing chances due to the weak pawn e3, although the passed pawn a5 is very dangerous, securing White advantage.}) 25. Rxb4 Qa7 ({After} 25... Qc7 26. Bd5 {White wins the pawn e4.}) 26. Qa2 $1 {[White forces the transfer to the ending with an extra pawn.]} Qxa2 27. Bxa2 Ba6 28. c4 h6 29. Rb6 Bc8 30. h3 ({White is not in hurry to win the pawn d6 - it will not run away. Although} 30. Nxf5 Rxf5 ({or} 30... Bxf5 31. Rbxd6 e3 32. fxe3 Rxe3 33. Rf1) 31. Rbxd6 {has also been possible.}) 30... Re5 31. Rd2 Be6 32. Nxf5 Rfxf5 33. Rbxd6 {[The triumph of the White strategy - he has won the pawn d6.]} e3 34. fxe3 Rxe3 {[But nevertheless White task to convert the material advantage remains uneasy - there is few material on board.]} 35. Kh2 (35. Rd8+ Kh7 36. Bb1 $4 {immediately, was not possible because of} Re1+ {, winning the bishop. Probably at this moment Black has already been in severe time-trouble. Only time shortage can explain why Boris Gelfand loses the ending without even creating White any problem.}) 35... Rfe5 36. Rd8+ Kh7 37. Bb1+ Bf5 38. Bxf5+ Rxf5 39. R8d5 {[#]} Rxd5 $4 {[Grave mistake, losing by force.]} ({White task could have still been very difficult after} 39... Rf6 40. c5 Rc6 $16) ({or} 39... Kg6 $16 {, planning to stop the advance of the pawn c4 with the king in the following line:} 40. Rxf5 Kxf5 41. Rc2 Ke6 42. c5 Kd7 43. c6+ Kc7) 40. cxd5 {[Now the game is over.]} Kg8 $6 ({More stubborn} 40... Re7 {still loses after} 41. d6 Rd7 42. Kg3 Kg6 43. Kg4 $18) 41. d6 Re8 42. Kg3 Kf7 43. d7 Rd8 44. Kg4 Kf6 45. h4 Ke6 46. Kh5 Kf6 47. Rf2+ Ke6 48. Kg6 1-0

